    <glyph id="COMPLEX_I" class="complex">
      <label text="Complex I" />
      <bbox x="130.00" y="137.44" w="154.93" h="60.24" />
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_I__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="FMN" />
        <bbox x="136.00" y="128.44" w="30.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_I__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="8 × Fe/S" />
        <bbox x="136.00" y="128.44" w="56.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:COMPLEX_I" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_2Fe2S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:58210</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G37510</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G08530</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G02580</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G11770</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005747</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">7.1.1.2</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The FMN site forms a flavin semiquinone during turnover and is a known superoxide-producing leak site, and the Fe/S chain performs sequential one-electron transfers — both of the physical routes the radical-pair and hyperfine arguments require. No published experiment has applied a near-null field and measured plant complex I activity, so this is a hypothesis with a mechanism, not a result.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Electron transfer along the Fe/S chain is fast relative to plausible singlet-triplet mixing times, so a coherent radical-pair effect here is harder to argue than at the flavin. Treat the flavin and the cluster chain as separate hypotheses rather than one.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="COMPLEX_IV" class="complex">
      <label text="Complex IV (COX)" />
      <bbox x="318.93" y="150.24" w="137.70" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_IV__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="heme a3 / CuB" />
        <bbox x="324.93" y="141.24" w="91.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:COMPLEX_IV" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>heme_copper_binuclear</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>cu_a_binuclear</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>heme_electron_carrier</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005751</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">7.1.1.9</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The review names cytochrome oxidase explicitly as a candidate direct metabolic target. The heme a3/CuB centre passes through paramagnetic oxygen intermediates (P, F, oxoferryl) whose spin states are, in principle, field-addressable.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Strongly exchange-coupled metal centres relax very fast, which argues against coherent spin effects. This is a mechanistically attractive but physically demanding hypothesis, and is labelled T3 for that reason.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="ACONITASE" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_matrix">
      <label text="Aconitase" />
      <bbox x="490.63" y="137.44" w="154.93" h="60.24" />
      <glyph id="ACONITASE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="4Fe-4S sentinel" />
        <bbox x="496.63" y="128.44" w="105.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:ACONITASE" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G35830</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G26970</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G05710</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">4.2.1.3</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>The review nominates aconitase as the sentinel of Fe/S integrity, and the biochemistry supports it: the exposed cluster iron makes aconitase the first TCA activity to fall under oxidative stress, so it reports cluster damage before anything else does.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Precisely because aconitase responds to any oxidative insult, a change under NNMF would NOT by itself implicate a magnetic mechanism. It is a sensitive readout and a poor discriminator; it needs a paired ROS-independent control.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="GERMINATION_ATP" class="simple chemical" compartmentRef="cell">
      <label text="ATP / ADP ratio" />
      <bbox x="69.39" y="294.08" w="120.35" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="GERMINATION_ATP__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="the missing measurement" />
        <bbox x="75.39" y="285.08" w="161.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:GERMINATION_ATP" evidenceTier="T4" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>structural_context</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:30616</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:456216</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="kegg">C00002</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="kegg">C00008</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Nietzel2020" doi="10.1073/pnas.1910501117" direction="increase">Mitochondrial energy metabolism is redox-kick-started during seed germination, establishing that respiratory competence is restored before photosynthesis.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Rosental2014" doi="10.1017/S0960258513000391" direction="increase">Primary metabolism is activated and regulated during seed germination, with respiration supplying the energy for reserve mobilisation and cell expansion.
</qbo:evidence>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="MITO_SUPEROXIDE" class="simple chemical" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_matrix">
      <label text="ROS signalling window" />
      <bbox x="223.73" y="287.68" w="169.45" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="MITO_SUPEROXIDE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="too little / too much" />
        <bbox x="229.73" y="278.68" w="147.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:MITO_SUPEROXIDE" evidenceTier="T2" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:18421</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="chebi">CHEBI:16240</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT3G10920</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Parmagnani2022" doi="10.3390/biom12121824" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">Near-null magnetic field exposure alters ROS-associated transcriptomic and metabolomic programmes in Arabidopsis.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Farooq2021" doi="10.3389/fpls.2021.781734" direction="altered">Reactive oxygen species and mitochondria jointly regulate seed germination, establishing the route from mitochondrial ROS to a developmental outcome.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>Not a magnetosensitive mechanism itself — it is the amplifier. A field effect too small to measure as a rate change can still register here, because ROS signalling is threshold-like.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="CYTOCHROME_C" class="macromolecule">
      <label text="Cytochrome c" />
      <bbox x="427.18" y="287.68" w="148.99" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="CYTOCHROME_C__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="→ ABI4 / ABA" />
        <bbox x="433.18" y="278.68" w="84.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:CYTOCHROME_C" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>heme_electron_carrier</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G22840</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G10040</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005758</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Racca2022" doi="10.1111/nph.18287" direction="altered">Cytochrome c and the transcription factor ABI4 link mitochondrial status to ABA-dependent seed germination.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>A one-electron heme carrier, paramagnetic in the ferric state. Included because Racca et al. place cytochrome c on the path from mitochondrial state to germination timing, which is the developmental window the review argues matters most.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="RADICLE_EMERGENCE" class="phenotype" compartmentRef="organism">
      <label text="Radicle emergence" />
      <bbox x="249.33" y="425.12" w="146.90" h="34.64" />
      <glyph id="RADICLE_EMERGENCE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="0–72 h endpoint" />
        <bbox x="255.33" y="416.12" w="105.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:RADICLE_EMERGENCE" evidenceTier="T4" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>structural_context</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Farooq2021" doi="10.3389/fpls.2021.781734" direction="altered">Reactive oxygen species and mitochondria jointly regulate seed germination, defining the window in which a respiratory lesion would have most leverage.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Eckardt2005" doi="10.1105/tpc.105.034843" direction="altered">Peroxisomal citrate synthase provides the exit route from fatty acid metabolism in oilseeds, linking reserve mobilisation to the TCA cycle.
</qbo:evidence>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
